      "role": "user",
      "content": "Read my Review Notes and the Beneficiary Designations for the Webb household. Did we miss any beneficiary updates, and what did we agree to act on?",
      "timestamp": "2026-06-13T09:12:00.000Z"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Here's what stands out, and yes, there's a real gap.\n\n**The beneficiary gap: the old 401(k).**\nYour beneficiary file shows the prior-employer 401(k) still lists Jessica Reyes (100%), dated 2019, with no contingent. Your review notes have Marcus saying he and his first wife divorced in 2019 and he \"updated everything.\" He didn't update this one. As it stands, if Marcus died before the rollover, that ~$96k would go to his ex-spouse, not to Tanya. Every other account already names Tanya (or Marcus) primary with the kids 50/50 contingent, so this is the single outlier. Confirm it against the custodian's record, not Marcus's memory.\n\n**What you agreed to act on (from the notes):**\n- Roll the old 401(k) into Marcus's IRA. This is the priority, and it also fixes the beneficiary problem above, the IRA's correct beneficiaries would then control.\n- Increase the joint brokerage auto-contribution by $400/month (Tanya's raise).\n- Revisit the Roth conversion before December.\n- Recheck every beneficiary designation against the custodian.\n\nI'm not making the change for you, just showing you where the record disagrees with what the client believes. Verify the old 401(k) beneficiary with the custodian before you rely on any of this.",
      "timestamp": "2026-06-13T09:14:00.000Z"
    }
  ]
}
